{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And HaShem commanded us to do all these statutes, to fear HaShem our Elohim.</b> He explains here the reward of the commandments, and says that the principal aim and intention for which we were commanded in all these statutes is the fear of HaShem, and it is the foundation of all the commandments. And when he said, <b>for our good always, that He might preserve us alive, as at this day,</b> he explained that the commandments contain benefit for body and for soul. 'For our good always' — this is the benefit of the soul in the world to come, for they alone are called 'days,' and this is what is written of the righteous (Genesis 24): ״בא בימים״ — 'well stricken in days' — in the language of knowledge (definite article), for he draws near to those long, eternal days, those of which David said (Psalms 34): ״מי האיש החפץ חיים אוהב ימים לראות טוב״ — 'Who is the man who desires life, who loves days, to see good' — for in those days a person merits the hidden good. 'That He might preserve us alive, as at this day' — the benefit of the body in this world. And he added in it the letter ה (in כהיום — 'as this') because this world was created with the letter ה. And the reason he set forth first the benefit of the soul and its superiority — and moreover because the world to come was created before this world. And afterward he said, ״וצדקה תהיה לנו לפני ה׳ אלהינו״ — 'and it shall be righteousness for us before HaShem our Elohim' — he added to explain that that good which shall be ours will be לפני ה׳ אלהינו, 'before HaShem our Elohim,' and called it צדקה (righteousness); this is what it says: ״וצדקה תהיה לנו לפני ה׳ אלהינו״ — and this is in the world of souls; and therefore Onkelos, of blessed memory, translated it: וזכו — 'and it shall be merit for us.'" "<b>And behold,</b> it has been explained here explicitly: the reward of the world to come, and this is the conclusion and seal of the answer to the question of the sons for all generations, so that they may believe and understand and know that there is in the performance of the commandments recompense and reward, both bodily and spiritual. The bodily is what he said: 'to give us the land' — which includes all the goodness of this world and its tranquility, in that it is a land flowing with milk and honey. The spiritual is what he said: 'for our good always' and 'and it shall be righteousness for us before HaShem our Elohim.' And behold, this is a great principle; you shall remember it always."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {0 "Hebrew text reads 'ומעלתו, ועוד כי העולם הבא נברא קודם העולם הזה' with no explicit subject for 'נברא'; translated hyper-literally, reading 'the world to come' as subject carried over from the prior clause."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:24.022669607Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}